00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve kaufman here  talking about language learning  talking about the contradictions  inherent in language learning if you  enjoy these videos please subscribe and  if you feel like it give me a like if  you enjoy this video so  recently i  made a couple of videos which generated  feedback which i always appreciate  one was on  pointing out that some of  elements of coercion in other words very  specific almost mechanical short-term  goals targets  like creating a hundred links in each of  the languages i'm studying  is very effective and enables me to work  my way through  content that i would other otherwise  find either too difficult or not very  interesting  so people said well you used to say you  know what happened to enjoying it and  comprehensible input so there's a  contradiction there  but  the explanation is first of all  i'm entitled to change my views on  things although i must say i've been  quite consistent what i wrote in my book  15 years ago before i had ever heard of  stephen krashen  still remains true and that is that the  best way to learn is to engage with  content of interest lots of listening  and reading however  the reality is that when you start in a  language  nothing is comprehensible so you can't  start with comprehensible input and  that's where we have our mini stories  that link with lots of repetition  uh verbs high frequency verbs changing  their forms  and i find that extremely useful to get  started in a language i didn't have many  stories 20 30 years ago even 10 years  ago so that has changed i believe in  those even though they're not inherently  intrinsically interesting or compelling  the second thing is once you have done  the many stories and that's where i am  in  arabic persian turkish  then  even though there are still areas that i  don't understand and things that i  continue to forget  still  it's a kind of a comfortable area to get  from that comfort zone into the stuff  that i really want to be able to  understand such as political discussions  on the internet is a long road and when  i'm in those difficult  uh transcribed podcasts for example with  lots of unknown words it's difficult it  can be frustrating and so then i have  had a tendency to go back to my mini  stories because i can do those and so i  found  that by forcing myself to create a  hundred links i was able to force myself  to plow forward into these inherently  difficult texts and i know from  experience that in time they will become  more comprehensible and more enjoyable  i also found that some of the content  that we had in our linked library which  wasn't inherently interesting but where  at least that transcript is accurate  because when i'm transcribing podcasts  very often the transcripts are not  accurate another element of frustration  so if i go back into  content in our link library in persian  or arabic  it may not be as interesting as the al  jazeera or franz van cat podcast  but if i say okay my goal is to save 100  links i can go in there and i find that  an  enjoyable activity the extent that i  achieve what i set out to do  and i know that in time i will be able  to enjoyably listen to the political  podcasts  i know that because for example when i  started into ukrainian i had nothing and  today with the events that you know the  impeachment  trial or whatever discussions in the u.s  senate  ukraine is in the news every day  and so i wanted to listen to a ukrainian  podcast so i went there and i just  understand it haven't listened to  ukrainian for months and months and  months and i go in there and i  understand it so that's kind of  reassuring you know if you put the  effort in because it used to be that the  ukrainian podcasts were as difficult to  understand as the arabic podcasts are  today so to that extent it's a  contradiction but i find it's a way of  pushing myself into more difficult  content  or even doing less interesting content  which is continuing then to have this  process of the the language  you know enter my brain so that's that  contradiction then there was a  contradiction over  uh how close can we get to the native  accent obviously our goal is to imitate  the native imitate how the native uses  words imitate the native pronunciation  the more we are in empathy with the  native the better we're going to learn  and the better our pronunciation will be  however i said it's unlikely that we  will be mistaken for a native and i say  that because  the only language i can judge is english  and in all my years of dealing with  people who are non-native speakers of  english  i don't think i've met one person who  learned english as an adult who  can be mistaken for a native such people  may exist and there are people in the  comment section saying i know so-and-so  and he or she  you know sounds just like a native maybe  maybe there are those people i haven't  met them  uh eventually something gives them away  it's like the old expression you can  fool some of the people all of the time  or all of the people some of the time  but you can't fool all of the people all  of the time so the native has so much so  much exposure to the language and it's  so natural to them that it to my mind  unrealistic to expect that you will  achieve that level of proficiency  including pronunciation  in another language that doesn't mean  that you shouldn't try and the closer  you get the better and obviously the  greater your empathy and the greater  your willingness to step out of your  native culture  the better you will do  so but there is a contradiction there we  try to be like the native and yet we're  unlikely to achieve it  another uh contradiction in a way is  that  the more we study something like  declension tables or the rules of the  subjunctive the more we try to nail it  down  the less well we learn it  if we spend our time  engaging with the language hearing the  language  uh moving around to different levels of  difficulty of content occasionally  looking at the rules or the tables  that's a faster path we will be more  successful at learning  all those nuts and bolts of the language  than if we  deliberately try to learn them so that  in a sense is another contradiction  another contradiction is  sort of contradiction uh people often  say well i understand well but i can't  speak and i find that not to be the case  even for those people who say that it is  the case  because in so many cases  i've challenged people who say that and  i say well you understand well then  can you comfortably read novels in the  language  the answer is no  do you understand movies no  i knew a swedish lady who spoke french  quite well  and so i gave her a gift of an audiobook  french literature she said she couldn't  understand it  so  when we  get our comprehension to a level where  we can read novels understand audiobooks  the speaking will come  and conversely many of the people who  say they don't speak well in fact speak  well enough  they're too critical of their own  efforts if we look at the goal of  language learning as being comprehension  first of all  and then the ability to communicate that  doesn't mean you have to communicate  with no mistakes or communicate in a  native like accent just communicate if  that's the goal then put your major  effort on comprehension because the  speaking will come an example of that is  our school system in canada where in the  traditional system they teach  the language they teach the grammar they  teach kids to speak  for 10 years and at the end of 10 years  they can't speak contradiction whereas  kids who are in french immersion like my  grandchildren they have all their  subjects in french  so the focus is on comprehension they  have to understand  whether it be social sciences or maths  or whatever physics in french  they don't speak very well i know my  grandchildren they don't speak french  they can kind of speak but they don't  speak very well but they have good  comprehension  and the many examples of immersion kids  who go to a french-speaking environment  and very quickly they speak very very  well so the contradiction in a sense is  if you want to speak well don't spend  your time trying to speak you're better  off to focus on comprehension and then  when you speak don't worry about it  um  yeah i can't remember i had a few other  things noted down  until fire sort of contradictions in  language learning  but there you have it i appreciate uh  getting your feedback  and i should also remind you that i do  have my uh 10 email course  10 tips on language learning if you  would like to receive this free of  charge please click on the link  and that's  what i had to say today and if there are  things that i say where i contradict  myself  i can always fall back  on  the statement do as i say not as i do  my teacher used to say that  bye for now

